// src/v4/std/collection.dag
//
// Scope: bounded containers — every collection grounds structurally in
//        an algebra from std/algebra.dag, so the collection-side
//        operations are reads of that algebra, never an enumerated
//        per-op table (the NO-ENGINE STOP per std/algebra.dag header
//        U1).
// Anchor: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_monoid (List/Map) +
//         https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algebra_of_sets (Set)
//
// Owns:
//   - List<T>        — finite sequences (the free-monoid carrier over T)
//   - Set<T>         — membership-predicate carrier
//   - MapEntry<K, V> — single (key, value) pair
//   - Map<K, V>      — finite associations (the free-monoid carrier
//                      over MapEntry<K, V>, with a uniqueness-of-keys
//                      discipline — see modeling notes)
//
// Each container's operations FALL OUT of its algebra grounding:
//   - List<T>      grounds in FreeMonoid<T> by the structural alias;
//                  fold ops read a Monoid<T> the call site supplies
//                  (the brief's "Monoid for fold").
//   - Set<T>       grounds in BooleanAlgebra<Set<T>> by the pointwise
//                  extension of Bool's BooleanAlgebra (union =
//                  pointwise OR, intersect = pointwise AND, complement
//                  = NOT, empty = constant false, universe = constant
//                  true).
//   - Map<K, V>    grounds in FreeMonoid<MapEntry<K, V>> by the
//                  structural alias; merge is the monoid op with a
//                  right-bias rule on duplicate keys (later occurrence
//                  wins — the standard map-merge semantics; the monoid
//                  laws hold because the op is associative and the
//                  empty entry list is identity).
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag: the Cardinality and Instantiation connectives are
//     how the kernel represents these parameterized container types in
//     the type DAG (no Cardinality TYPE is consumed — see scaffold
//     note below).
//   - std/algebra.dag: FreeMonoid<T> (the recursive carrier List/Map
//     ground in by carrier identity — the same grounding move
//     algebra.dag's header anticipates: "Concrete sequence types
//     ground by aliasing onto it"). BooleanAlgebra<T> grounds Set
//     pointwise but appears here only as a header reference, not as a
//     parametric data instance (see modeling notes).
//   (Bool and the type parameters T, K, V are kernel-ambient; Symbol
//   is substrate-ambient per std/node.dag K-1 — none is imported.)
//
// Cardinality refinement — TRACKED SCAFFOLD (🟡):
//   The scaffold header named the family "List<T, n: Cardinality>,
//   Set<T, n>, Map<K, V, n>" — a refinement carrier that surfaces an
//   exact element count on the type. With std/cardinality.dag still
//   in flight (#3163, T-3 Wave-A2), no Cardinality TYPE is consumable
//   yet; only the Cardinality CONNECTIVE in std/node.dag exists.
//   Today's modeling treats cardinality as INTRINSIC to the carrier
//   (length-of-FreeMonoid for List/Map; count-of-`true`-elements for
//   Set), not as a refinement parameter — the three bridge
//   properties:
//     (a) scaffold doc — THIS note; cardinality is read off the
//         carrier shape, not declared on the type.
//     (b) bounds on use — pipeline stages reading a Cardinality fact
//         derive it by walking the FreeMonoid spine (List/Map) or by
//         folding `member` against an enumerable T (Set, where T's
//         enumerability is itself a structural fact).
//     (c) dissolution trigger — when std/cardinality.dag lands its
//         refinement substrate, the carrier-derived count becomes a
//         refinement read at the type level (`List<T, n>`,
//         `Set<T, n>`, `Map<K, V, n>`) and this scaffold note
//         dissolves.
//
// NO ENGINE — NO ENUMERATED OPS. There is deliberately no
// `list_concat` / `set_union` / `map_lookup` table here: those would
// be a parallel representation of structure std/algebra.dag already
// owns (the `kernel_algebra_profile` debt v3 carried, which
// std/algebra.dag's U1 note explicitly forbids — "not a name-keyed
// table lookup"). Consumers read the algebra structures from
// algebra.dag and the carrier shapes here; ops are reads of that one
// authority, not table rows.
//
// Status: T-3 modeled 2026-05-16 (witty-dove-121). This header is the
// I/O contract downstream workers build to; splitting this file
// requires explicit operator ratification (substrate extension = stop
// signal).


module v4.std.collection


import v4.std.algebra { FreeMonoid }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// CARRIERS, NOT STRUCTURES. This file owns CONTAINER CARRIERS. The
// algebra structures they ground in (Monoid, BooleanAlgebra) are owned
// by std/algebra.dag; the inhabitant owns its OWN grounding here per
// std/algebra.dag's SCOPE note. The grounding is the structural
// alias / pointwise lift — there is no per-carrier `data
// <name>: <Algebra><Carrier> = <Algebra> { … }` instance, for two
// reasons that compose:
//   (i)  v2-data instance-values are not parametric — no precedent for
//        `data list_T_monoid<T>: Monoid<List<T>> = Monoid { … }` in
//        the v2 seed (the closest precedent is concrete-typed `data
//        timing_lens_monoid: Monoid<TimingMeasurement>` in v3
//        timing_lens.dag), and a parametric instance would need a
//        substrate the seed doesn't yet have.
//   (ii) Even if it did, a stored instance would be a SECOND AUTHORITY
//        for the monoid the FreeMonoid carrier already IS (INVARIANTS
//        P2 — the std/algebra.dag header U1's "engine debt" — a
//        name-keyed table lookup mirroring the structure it traces).
// The aliasing IS the inhabitance — the same pattern std/text.dag
// uses for `String = FreeMonoid<Char>`.
//
// NO NEW COPRODUCTS. Every declaration below is either a TYPE ALIAS
// onto a coproduct owned elsewhere (List, Map → FreeMonoid in
// algebra.dag) or a Conj record (Set, MapEntry). No new Disj declared
// here means no new Practice-4 5-pattern ledger applies — the
// FreeMonoid<T> ledger already lives in algebra.dag and is the one
// authority.
//
// OPS FALL OUT OF GROUNDING — illustrated, not enumerated. The header
// names which algebra each carrier grounds in and how the ops project.
// Authoring those projections as concrete functions here would be the
// per-op table the brief forbids; they are pipeline-stage / lens reads
// of (carrier shape) ⊗ (algebra structure), consumed at the use site.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// List<T> — finite sequences of T. Structurally identical to
// FreeMonoid<T> (Empty | Cons { head, tail }); the alias IS the
// inhabitance — by being a FreeMonoid<T>, List<T> is a free-monoid
// carrier and inherits the Monoid structure with no parallel data
// instance (std/algebra.dag header: "Concrete sequence types ground
// by aliasing onto it (e.g. a text type onto FreeMonoid over a char
// type)").
//
// Operations (concat, length, fold, map, filter, head, tail) are
// STRUCTURAL READS of the FreeMonoid Empty/Cons constructors composed
// with a Monoid<T> the CALL SITE supplies — never enumerated here.
// The element type's algebra is the only authority for the per-call
// reduction; the carrier shape is the only authority for the
// recursion.
//
// This declaration is a type ALIAS onto a coproduct owned by
// std/algebra.dag (the FreeMonoid<T> = Empty | Cons ledger lives
// there). No new Practice-4 ledger applies — there is no new
// coproduct to dissolve at this layer.
type List<T> = FreeMonoid<T>


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// MapEntry<K, V> — one (key, value) pair. Conj record (no Practice-4
// ledger; records do not dissolve to a richer source). MapEntry is
// the element of the Map<K, V> free monoid; the key is treated as
// MEMBERSHIP IDENTITY (uniqueness discipline lives on Map, not on
// the entry — see Map's modeling note).
type MapEntry<K, V> {
  key: K
  value: V
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Map<K, V> — finite associations from K to V. Modeled as the free
// monoid over MapEntry<K, V>: an ordered sequence of entries; the
// monoid op is concatenation with a right-bias rule on duplicate keys
// (later occurrence wins — the standard map-merge semantics). The
// monoid laws hold because concatenation is associative and the empty
// entry list is identity.
//
// Uniqueness-of-keys is a SEMANTIC discipline producers honor, not a
// type-enforced invariant in v4 today — the refinement substrate that
// would express it lives in std/cardinality.dag + std/witness.dag
// (both T-3, in flight; collection.dag is Wave-A1 by the operator's
// split, ahead of them). The grounded definition + its dissolution
// trigger live in the Cardinality scaffold note in the file header
// above; once the refinement substrate lands, `Map<K, V>` becomes a
// `FreeMonoid<MapEntry<K, V>>` refined by `unique_keys`, and the
// uniqueness obligation moves from producer-side to type-enforced.
//
// As with List, this is a type ALIAS onto algebra.dag's
// FreeMonoid<…> coproduct; no new Practice-4 ledger applies here.
type Map<K, V> = FreeMonoid<MapEntry<K, V>>


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Set<T> — finite subsets of T, modeled as a membership-predicate
// carrier. A `Set<T>` IS its `member` function: x ∈ S iff
// `S.member(x) == true`. This is the textbook denotational form
// (Wikipedia "Algebra of sets"): a set is a function T → Bool.
//
// Algebra grounding — Set<T> inhabits BooleanAlgebra<Set<T>> by
// POINTWISE EXTENSION of Bool's BooleanAlgebra:
//
//   union(a, b)              ≡ member: λx. a.member(x) || b.member(x)
//   intersect(a, b)          ≡ member: λx. a.member(x) && b.member(x)
//   complement(a)            ≡ member: λx. ! a.member(x)
//   empty                    ≡ member: λ_. false                (bottom)
//   universe                 ≡ member: λ_. true                 (top)
//   is_subset(a, b)          ≡ ∀x. a.member(x) → b.member(x)
//   difference(a, b)         ≡ intersect(a, complement(b))
//   symmetric_difference     ≡ union(difference(a,b), difference(b,a))
//
// Every one of these falls out of the Bool BooleanAlgebra owned by
// std/algebra.dag composed with this carrier — there is deliberately
// no `data set_T_boolean_algebra: BooleanAlgebra<Set<T>>` instance
// here (the parametric-data + single-authority reasoning in the
// modeling notes). The pointwise lift IS the inhabitance, exactly the
// algebra.dag "aliasing is the inhabitant's concern" pattern.
//
// Conj record (one field, `member`); not a coproduct — no Practice-4
// ledger applies.
type Set<T> {
  member: fn(T) -> Bool
}
